WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.141
Weak passwords and poor recovery workflows make account takeover easier.

2
00:00:04.141 --> 00:00:12.772
Password policy, breached-password checks, reset flows, and administrative recovery processes all affect authentication strength.

3
00:00:12.772 --> 00:00:22.270
MFA failures occur when multifactor authentication is missing, optional, bypassable, or undermined by weak recovery and exception processes.

4
00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:27.040
Privileged access and sensitive tenant operations require particular attention.

5
00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:32.188
Session management determines whether authenticated access remains trustworthy after login.

6
00:00:32.188 --> 00:00:40.290
Tokens should be protected, rotated when appropriate, invalidated after logout or major changes, and resistant to replay.

7
00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:51.535
Credential attacks such as credential stuffing, password spraying, and brute force attempts require rate limits, lockout logic, monitoring, alerting, and risk-based checks.

8
00:00:51.535 --> 00:00:55.686
Use multi-factor authentication for privileged and sensitive access.

9
00:00:55.686 --> 00:00:59.388
Handle sessions with secure token practices and timeouts.

10
00:00:59.388 --> 00:01:02.940
Apply strong password policies and credential monitoring.

11
00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:06.961
Selected SOC 2 mappings for this topic include CC6.

12
00:01:06.961 --> 00:01:10.014
1 - Access controls; CC6.

13
00:01:10.014 --> 00:01:11.960
2 - Authentication mechanisms.
